  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Chef has two **distinct** positive integers AAA and BBB.\n\nChef wonders how many distinct values are possible for the expression gcd(A+X,B+X)\\mathrm{gcd}(A+X,B+X)gcd(A+X,B+X), where XXX can take any non-negative integer value.\n\nHelp Chef find this value.\n\nHere, gcd\\mathrm{gcd}gcd stands for Greatest Common Divisor.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line contains a single integer TTT — the number of test cases. Then the test cases follow.\nThe first and only line of each test case contains two distinct space separated integers AAA and BBB.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each testcase, output the number of distinct values of the expression gcd(A+X,B+X)\\mathrm{gcd}(A+X,B+X)gcd(A+X,B+X), where XXX can take any non-negative integer value.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤10001 \\leq T \\leq 10001≤T≤1000",
      "1≤A,B≤1091 \\leq A, B \\leq 10^91≤A,B≤109",
      "A≠BA \\neq BA=B"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "2\n1 2\n12 8",
        "output": "1\n3",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 1: Here gcd(1+X,2+X)=1\\mathrm{gcd}(1+X, 2+X) = 1gcd(1+X,2+X)=1 no matter what value of XXX you choose.\nTest case 2:\n\nIf we take X=0X = 0X=0, gcd(12,8)=4\\mathrm{gcd}(12, 8) = 4gcd(12,8)=4.\nIf we take X=1X = 1X=1, gcd(13,9)=1\\mathrm{gcd}(13, 9) = 1gcd(13,9)=1.\nIf we take X=2X = 2X=2, gcd(14,10)=2\\mathrm{gcd}(14, 10) = 2gcd(14,10)=2.\n\nIt can be shown that no other value of gcd\\mathrm{gcd}gcd is possible."
      }
    ]
  },
